+# Decision: Reconciling Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A plot visit is edited offline on shared tablets, and copies of the same visit are corrected independently after field notes are compared. Two submissions can descend from the same starting state days apart, with no network ordering that makes one more authoritative. The record store must define what a current visit is when such copies arrive (record-store-design.md). The chosen mechanism fixes the submission envelope tablets build, the shape of the review work two coordinators perform, and which visits are eligible for the partner's Friday CSV, so tablet-sync and review-export are sized around it.
+
+Both candidate mechanisms retain original submissions. The tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit" is therefore a standing constraint every option satisfies; it is background to this decision, not a point of selection. What is at stake is the opposite failure: an exported current record that no single worker authored as a coherent observation, or a review queue the two coordinators cannot clear before Friday.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Both options clear the standing constraint above, so no binary gate eliminates either; the decision turns on weighted criteria. Importance uses major / moderate / minor.
+
+- **Scientific coherence of the exported record** (major). Derived from the goal "Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export" and the non-goal "Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent." A current record assembled field by field can pair edits that are scientifically related — a species identification with its cover estimate — without any worker having authored that pairing. Field-level provenance keeps each value traceable but does not flag the pairing, so the result reads as a plausible observation and the problem is not surfaced for review. The system cannot be asked to detect the relationship, because botanical inference is a non-goal. A silent scientific error in the partner's data is the costliest failure mode.
+- **Coordinator review load** (major). Derived from the tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator." Two coordinators resolve questions across roughly 6,000 visits per season. Review volume is a weekly, bounded cost that must fit their capacity.
+- **Operational maintenance burden** (moderate). Derived from the same tenet. Logic that needs authored rules kept current as the record schema changes is a long-term cost a two-person team must carry, but it is not a weekly one.
+- **Export readiness** (moderate). Derived from the goals "Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export" and "Export the partner's existing columns without requiring spreadsheet cleanup." More visits resolved without review means more rows in each Friday CSV. A visit held out this week appears in a later one.
+- **Submission size and tablet state** (minor). Derived from the tenet "Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity." At the scale of structured records in the first release, differences in payload size and retained local state are negligible.
+
+Two further candidate criteria score equally on both options and carry no weight: ease of undoing a resolution (both retain originals) and the review UI surface each needs (both require one). One latent criterion does move the decision: whether the review queue stays within two coordinators' capacity as visit volume and concurrent-edit rate grow. It is carried in Selection as a reconsideration trigger, since it is a condition on the decision's durability rather than a difference between the options today.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission names the base revision it was derived from and carries a complete snapshot of the visit. A second submission derived from the same base is retained as a competing whole revision; the visit has no single current state until a coordinator selects one of the competing revisions as current. No field-level combination is performed — every candidate for current is exactly one worker's submitted visit. Tablets track a base revision ID rather than a base snapshot. Review compares complete revisions and holds unresolved visits out of the export. The server-side logic is base-lineage comparison and coordinator selection only.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and Worker B both fetch revision R2. A corrects an observer name (R3, base R2); B adds a missing species (R4, base R2). R3 and R4 are competing revisions. The record store returns an unresolved marker listing R3 and R4; a coordinator compares the two whole visits and selects, say, R3 as the current revision. R4 and every original submission remain in history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific coherence of the exported record: **strong** — the current record is always one worker's coherent visit; the system never pairs a species change with a cover change from a different worker.
+- Coordinator review load: **weak** — even unrelated edits (an observer name, a missing species) produce a competing pair a coordinator must clear; every concurrent pair adds one review item.
+- Operational maintenance burden: **strong** — no merge rules to author or maintain; only base-lineage comparison and selection.
+- Export readiness: **weak** — unresolved visits are ineligible and wait for a later CSV.
+- Submission size and tablet state: **partial** — a complete snapshot is a larger payload, but tablet state is a single base revision ID; negligible at this scale.
+
+**Summary.** A larger, predictable review queue in exchange for a current record that is always one worker's intact observation.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries the base snapshot it was derived from plus structured, per-field changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically into a current record; only overlapping changes to the same field go to review. A current record can therefore be assembled from several workers' edits, with field-level provenance recording which submission contributed each value. Tablets retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review examines overlapping changes and per-field provenance. Merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries are authored and maintained, and revisited whenever the record schema changes.
+
+**Why it stays a live alternative.** Most independent corrections genuinely are unrelated — an observer-name fix and a missing species are common and safe to combine. Auto-combining them keeps the review queue small, gets more visits export-ready for each Friday deadline, and asks the two coordinators to look only at real conflicts. At higher visit volumes or concurrent-edit rates, this is the difference between a queue that clears and one that does not. It is the better mechanism whenever coordinator review capacity, rather than scientific coherence, is the binding constraint.
+
+**Demonstration.** From base R2, Worker A changes the observer list and Worker B adds a species entry. The merge produces a current record containing both changes with provenance per field and no review item. If both also edit the same species' cover estimate, that overlap is flagged for a coordinator.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific coherence of the exported record: **weak** — the merge rules operate on field identities, not scientific relationships. A species-identification change and its cover estimate, made by different workers from the same base, are disjoint fields and would be combined into a record no worker authored as a whole; because the combination is internally plausible, an overlap review does not reliably expose it.
+- Coordinator review load: **strong** — only genuine field overlaps reach review; disjoint edits create no work.
+- Operational maintenance burden: **weak** — authored, maintained rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries, plus field-level provenance throughout, all revisited as the schema changes.
+- Export readiness: **strong** — more visits are auto-resolved and eligible for the current Friday CSV.
+- Submission size and tablet state: **partial** — structured changes are smaller in transit, but tablets retain full base snapshots.
+
+**Summary.** Maintained merge logic and a blind spot for scientifically related edits in exchange for a small review queue and high export readiness.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: Whole-Visit Revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the major criterion of scientific coherence against the major criterion of coordinator review load, with the tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" and the tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator" pulling in opposite directions. Field-aware three-way merge is genuinely strong on the second tenet — it is the better mechanism when review capacity is the binding constraint — and whole-visit revisions is chosen against that advantage, not because the advantage is absent. It loses because its weakness on scientific coherence is the failure the project can least afford: a merged record that silently pairs scientifically related edits looks like a valid observation, so neither the merge rules nor a reviewer of overlaps reliably catches it, and the non-goal "Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent" rules out asking the system to detect the relationship. A larger review queue is visible, bounded work two coordinators can schedule before Friday; a quietly wrong exported value is neither. The moderate criteria reinforce this: whole-visit revisions carries no maintained merge rules, while the export-readiness edge of field-aware merging is a timeliness gain — a held-out visit appears in a later CSV, whereas a wrong value propagates to the science partner. This is a deliberate acceptance of more coordinator review work in exchange for a guarantee about the exported record's provenance, not a claim that whole-visit revisions dominates on every element.
+
+The decision is taken on the tenets "Do not silently discard a submitted edit" and "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" in their strongest form — the current record is always one intact, retained worker submission — against the coordinator-load advantage field-aware merging draws from "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator."
+
+**Foreclosed.** Automatic combination of unrelated field edits, field-level provenance in the current record, and the lower review volume those bring. A later move to field-aware merging requires either accepting the scientific-coherence risk or first obtaining reliable detection of scientific relationships between fields, which the current non-goal excludes.
+
+**Obligations downstream (cascades of this decision, owned by the sibling segments).** The contract itself is fixed in the record-store LLD; these are what the related work must accommodate.
+- tablet-sync retains the base revision ID of the last known current revision (not a full base snapshot), sends a complete visit snapshot with that base ID on every submission, builds its retry and idempotency state machine around this envelope, and surfaces pending competing revisions to field workers so an offline worker understands an edit is one of N competing revisions rather than the current record. It consumes this contract and defines no conflict semantics of its own.
+- review-export provides a revision-comparison screen over the unresolved marker's competing complete revisions, lets a coordinator select a winning revision, derives export eligibility from the record store's resolved/unresolved state rather than its own rules, and holds export-ineligible visits out of the partner's Friday CSV. It consumes the eligibility state and unresolved marker; it does not decide what makes a visit resolved.
+
+**Reconsideration triggers.** Re-run this judgment if (a) concurrent-edit volume grows to where two coordinators cannot clear the whole-visit review queue before the Friday export; (b) reliable detection of scientific relationships between fields becomes available without violating the non-goal; or (c) the record schema stabilizes enough that maintained merge rules become cheap to keep current. Under those conditions the coordinator-load and export-readiness advantages of field-aware three-way merge could outweigh the scientific-coherence cost.
